[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Configuration Changes and State Restoration in Android Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When a device undergoes a configuration change, such as screen rotation, language change, or keyboard availability alteration, the Android system destroys the current Activity and recreates it. While this mechanism ensures the application matches the new resource qualifiers, it poses a challenge for maintaining UI state and user input. This tutorial explores how to manage state restoration effectively using Java."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Lifecycle of Configuration Changes"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"During rotation, the operating system calls the normal lifecycle methods to tear down the Activity (onPause, onStop, onDestroy) and then invokes onCreate, onStart, and onResume to build it anew. Any transient data stored solely in local variables within the Activity will be lost unless explicitly saved before destruction."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Using onSaveInstanceState for Primitive Data"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"To persist lightweight UI states, such as text inside an EditText or a scroll position, override the onSaveInstanceState(Bundle) method. This method is called before the Activity is killed, allowing you to store key-value pairs into the provided Bundle."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"@Override
protected void onSaveInstanceState(Bundle outState) {
    super.onSaveInstanceState(outState);
    outState.putString("USER_INPUT_KEY", mEditText.getText().toString());
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Restoring State in onCreate or onRestoreInstanceState"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can restore the saved state either by checking if the savedInstanceState Bundle is null inside the onCreate method, or by utilizing the dedicated onRestoreInstanceState callback, which is called after onStart."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"@Override
protected void onCreate(Bundle savedInstanceState) {
    super.onCreate(savedInstanceState);
    setContentView(R.layout.activity_main);

    mEditText = findViewById(R.id.my_edit_text);

    if (savedInstanceState != null) {
        String savedString = savedInstanceState.getString("USER_INPUT_KEY");
        mEditText.setText(savedString);
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for State Management"},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Only use onSaveInstanceState for lightweight data like IDs, simple strings, or boolean flags."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid storing large datasets, bitmaps, or complex objects in the Bundle, as it may cause TransactionTooLargeException."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Leverage the Android Architecture Components ViewModel to handle heavier business logic and data persistence across configuration changes."}]
